Run:  python3 book_demo.py              (prints the whole book)
      python3 book_demo.py 3            (prints chapter 3 only)
      python3 book_demo.py search WORD  (finds WORD across chapters)
      python3 book_demo.py html         (writes book.html)
"""
import json, sys, html as _h

BOOK = {'title': 'Six-Guns and a strongbox key', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Stagecoach Runs', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Dust on the Wind', 'text': "But a strongbox key changed everything. When Nina Frost showed it — a strongbox key, genuine, legal, undeniable — the room went quiet. It was proof. Proof that the corrupt judge's claim was a lie, and lies, in Dust Creek, have a short life.\n\nthe ranch foreman was the first to offer a meal and the news. 'the corrupt judge means to take it all,' the ranch foreman said. 'The water, the grazing, the town. Unless somebody stands up.' Nina Frost ate slowly. Standing up was a habit.\n\nThat night in the saloon, the corrupt judge's men made their play: cards, insults, a gun cleared half out of leather. Nina Frost ended it without firing — the fast peace, the ranch foreman called it later, shaking their head."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Claim', 'text': "Thursday night they came with fire. The livery burned, and a strongbox key's copies with it — but Nina Frost had the original where fire couldn't reach. the corrupt judge had played the last card. Friday would tell.\n\nthe ranch foreman stood with Nina Frost — and then the blacksmith, and the schoolteacher, and one by one half the town. 'We know Nina Frost's word,' the sheriff said. It wasn't much. It was everything.\n\nThe judge — the corrupt judge's judge — set a hearing for Friday. Three days to prove a strongbox key real, with the corrupt judge's men watching every step. Nina Frost smiled the thin smile of someone holding four aces."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'High Noon', 'text': "Friday noon. The street emptied the way streets do before a storm. Nina Frost walked it alone — the ranch foreman had argued, but some walks you take solo. a strongbox key was in the courthouse. This was about more than paper.\n\nthe corrupt judge's claim died that afternoon, in public, on the record. Dust Creek exhaled. the ranch foreman bought the drinks. Nina Frost drank one, slow, tasting justice. It tasted like dust and sweet water.\n\nThe hearing went ahead anyway, because Nina Frost insisted on it. Law, not guns. a strongbox key was examined, the witness testified, the seal was verified. The judge — even the corrupt judge's judge — couldn't lie with the whole town watching."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "the corrupt judge wasn't done — the breed never is. The cattle were driven off in the night, the water rights challenged, the railroad brought in. Dust Creek needed more than a verdict; it needed a future.\n\nthe corrupt judge's rustlers hit them at the canyon — the last play of a losing hand. The fight was short and decisive: the town rode together, and together is a word the corrupt judge never understood.\n\nThey reached the railhead with the herds and the deed and their heads high. Dust Creek was solvent. Dust Creek was free. Nina Frost looked back at the dust of the trail and felt the peculiar ache of a job done."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "the corrupt judge was last seen heading east, smaller in the distance. Dust Creek didn't gloat. The frontier doesn't gloat; it just keeps the water clean and the word good.\n\na strongbox key stayed in the courthouse, under glass, with the iron likeness beside it. Proof. Promise. the frontier makes family — carved, at the ranch foreman's insistence, on the frame.\n\nBut Nina Frost stayed through the winter, helping the ranch foreman mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of Dust Creek instead of just its defender. It suited them."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "Dust Creek is still there, in the valley, under the big sky. The water runs. The cattle graze. The deed holds. Travelers ask about the iron a strongbox key in the courthouse, and the old-timers tell it true.\n\nthe frontier makes family. In Dust Creek, on the trail, at sundown and sunup. The west remembers.\n\nNina Frost rides somewhere west, where the map goes blank. There are always more valleys, more villains, more folks needing one person to stand. The frontier doesn't end. It just moves."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

