er.\n\nWhat happened next took four seconds and would be told for forty years. When the dust settled, the rustler king's gunmen were down — winged, not killed; Nina Frost never shot to kill if winging would do — and the rustler king stood alone, empty-handed."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "the rustler king's rustlers hit them at the canyon — the last play of a losing hand. The fight was short and decisive: the town rode together, and together is a word the rustler king never understood.\n\nNina Frost organized the drive: every ranch, every hand, moving the herds to the railhead themselves, cutting out the rustler king's middlemen. the schoolteacher rode drag. a strongbox key — the deed — rode in Nina Frost's breast pocket, the reason for it all.\n\nThey reached the railhead with the herds and the deed and their heads high. Red Mesa was solvent. Red Mesa was free. Nina Frost looked back at the dust of the trail and felt the peculiar ache of a job done."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "Red Mesa threw a dance. the schoolteacher danced every dance. The schoolteacher cried. The blacksmith forged a strongbox key's likeness in iron for the courthouse wall — 'so nobody forgets,' he said.\n\nNina Frost was offered the sheriff's star, the mayor's office, and three ranches. Nina Frost took none of them. 'I ride,' Nina Frost said, which explained everything and nothing, the way true things do.\n\nBut Nina Frost stayed through the winter, helping the schoolteacher mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of Red Mesa instead of just its defender. It suited them."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "Spring came, and Nina Frost rode out with the thaw, a strongbox key's story told and retold in the saloon. the schoolteacher watched them go from the porch, and didn't wave — waving is for endings, and this wasn't one.\n\nRed Mesa is still there, in the valley, under the big sky. The water runs. The cattle graze. The deed holds.